Goal: add a `--tsv` flag to the csv2json CLI so it reads tab-separated input from stdin. Make one pull request that meets every acceptance line below.

Current state (read these files first):
- bin/csv2json.js reads all of stdin and prints `JSON.stringify(parse(text))` followed by `\n`. It ignores argv.
- src/parse.js exports `parse(text, sep = ',')`. It splits rows on `\r?\n` and fields naively on `sep`. It already supports a custom separator.
- test/csv.test.js uses `node:test`. It has a `parse` test and a `cli` test that runs `execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js'], { input, encoding: 'utf8' })` from the repo root. package.json has `"test": "node --test"`.
- README.md documents only `node bin/csv2json.js < table.csv`.

Steps:
1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether `process.argv.slice(2)` includes `--tsv` anywhere. If it does, call `parse(text, '\t')`. Otherwise call `parse(text)` exactly as now. Keep the output format unchanged: `JSON.stringify(...)` plus one trailing `\n`. Do not change src/parse.js unless you have to. Do not add quoting rules, separator auto-detection, a general --sep/--delimiter option, --help, or new handling for unknown arguments.
2. In test/csv.test.js (or a new test/*.js file using node:test), add tests that run bin/csv2json.js with `--tsv` on tab-separated input and assert the exact stdout. Cover at least:
   - input `a\tb\n1\t2\n` -> `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]\n`
   - input `name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n` -> `[{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]\n`
   - input `a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n` -> `[{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]\n`
   - without the flag, input `a\tb\n1\t2\n` -> `[{"a\\tb":"1\\t2"}]\n`, which is the JSON tab escape (the current behaviour).
   Keep the existing tests passing.
3. In README.md, document the flag. Include an example invocation such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and say that `--tsv` reads tab-separated input. Keep the existing CSV usage.
4. Check the work from the repo root:
   - `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo "exit=$?"` prints `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]` and then `exit=0`.
   - `printf 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints `[{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]`.
   - `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints `[{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]`.
   - `printf 'a,b\n1,2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]`.
   - `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints `[{"a\tb":"1\t2"}]`, where `\t` is the literal two-character JSON escape.
   - `npm test` exits 0.

Acceptance lines (all must hold):
1. `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.
2. With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]` followed by a newline.
3. With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]` followed by a newline.
4. Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\n1,2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]` followed by a newline.
5. Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{"a\tb":"1\t2"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.
6. `npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.
7. README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation.